WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.188 --> 00:00:02.283
- [Narrator] In upstate New York

00:00:02.283 --> 00:00:03.811
there are two guys creating some dangerous

00:00:03.811 --> 00:00:05.986
and risky explosions.

00:00:05.986 --> 00:00:08.236
All in the name of science.

00:00:09.118 --> 00:00:11.868
(dramatic music)

00:00:14.709 --> 00:00:16.537
This is Ingo.

00:00:16.537 --> 00:00:17.531
- I am Ingo Sonder.

00:00:17.531 --> 00:00:20.321
I work for the Center
for Geohazard Studies

00:00:20.321 --> 00:00:22.168
at the University of Buffalo.

00:00:22.168 --> 00:00:23.476
- [Narrator] And this is Andrew.

00:00:23.476 --> 00:00:24.843
- My name is Andrew Harp.

00:00:24.843 --> 00:00:28.683
I am a PhD student at the
University of Buffalo.

00:00:28.683 --> 00:00:30.355
- [Narrator] They are researchers studying

00:00:30.355 --> 00:00:31.653
large scale disasters,

00:00:31.653 --> 00:00:34.723
more specifically volcanic eruptions.

00:00:34.723 --> 00:00:37.099
- [Andrew] Currently we
work on an experiment on

00:00:37.099 --> 00:00:40.254
explosive magma water interaction.

00:00:40.254 --> 00:00:41.397
- [Narrator] But there's a problem.

00:00:41.397 --> 00:00:44.533
There's no lava in upstate New York.

00:00:44.533 --> 00:00:46.450
So they made their own.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:48.902
They start with rocks.

00:00:48.902 --> 00:00:51.446
- [Ingo] 50 to 60 kilograms of rock.

00:00:51.446 --> 00:00:53.285
We melt it in our furnace.

00:00:53.285 --> 00:00:56.463
This takes four hours roughly.

00:00:56.463 --> 00:00:57.471
- [Narrator] The rock liquifies

00:00:57.471 --> 00:01:01.103
and can reach more than
2,300 degrees fahrenheit.

00:01:01.103 --> 00:01:03.999
- [Ingo] We then pour the melted rock

00:01:03.999 --> 00:01:06.535
from that furnace into a container

00:01:06.535 --> 00:01:09.799
and into that container we inject water

00:01:09.799 --> 00:01:13.808
and hope that the water
interacts dramatically

00:01:13.808 --> 00:01:15.558
with our molten rock.

00:01:16.705 --> 00:01:19.153
- [Andrew] It's almost
impossible without being there

00:01:19.153 --> 00:01:22.297
to understand how much heat is

00:01:22.297 --> 00:01:24.047
put off by this lava.

00:01:26.478 --> 00:01:30.250
- [Ingo] I have to wear
an aluminized suit,

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:33.066
a jacket and kind of pants.

00:01:33.066 --> 00:01:35.085
I have to wear that for protection

00:01:35.085 --> 00:01:37.002
from potential splashes

00:01:37.923 --> 00:01:40.356
and most importantly
from the heat radiation

00:01:40.356 --> 00:01:42.427
that comes out of there.

00:01:42.427 --> 00:01:45.587
We want to understand better
a magma water interaction

00:01:45.587 --> 00:01:48.011
and that actually occurs

00:01:48.011 --> 00:01:51.171
in explosive volcanic eruptions.

00:01:51.171 --> 00:01:52.058
- [Andrew] The goal would be

00:01:52.058 --> 00:01:56.141
to be able to predict
exactly the size and timing

00:01:57.008 --> 00:01:59.425
of the reaction that we have.

00:02:00.943 --> 00:02:04.095
I feel fortunate to be
working with something

00:02:04.095 --> 00:02:05.239
that's so unique

00:02:05.239 --> 00:02:06.640
that it's only done in a few places

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.376
in the entire world right now,

00:02:08.376 --> 00:02:12.543
and to be at the forefront
of this research is great.

